ese values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL194: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite51†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL195: \nL196: cite52†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite53†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL197: \nL198:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L199:   * cite4†Help†info.arxiv.org L200: \nL201:   * contact arXiv Click here to contact arXiv cite54† Contact†info.arxiv.org L202:   * subscribe to arXiv mailings Click here to subscribe cite55† Subscribe†info.arxiv.org L203:   * cite56†Copyright†info.arxiv.org L204:   * cite57†Privacy Policy†info.arxiv.org L205: \nL206:   * cite58†Web Accessibility Assistance†info.arxiv.org L207:   * cite59†arXiv Operational Status †status.arxiv.org L208: \nL209:   *[Related DOI]: Digital Object Identifier\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2841view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12032v1\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCPR: Mitigating Large Language Model Hallucinations with Curative Prompt Refinement (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12029v1)\nciteturn2841view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12029v1\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 311\nL76: While effective in reducing hallucinations, these methods have limitations in high computational demands and complex implementation processes, making them costly and challenging to scale [cite43†16 ].\nL77: ### II-B Prompt Refinement\nL78: Traditional prompt refinement strategies encompass various approaches, including the use of large LLMs, direct human intervention, and reinforcement learning [cite44†17 , cite45†18 ]. These methods typically aim to optimize prompts for a specific target LLM, often encompassing high computational costs. In contrast, our method utilizes a lightweight SLM for prompt refinement, designed to mitigate hallucinations across any LLM.\nL79: This positions the SLM as a more accessible and resource-efficient option for prompt optimization, achieving high levels of performance through task-specific fine-tuning, despite its smaller scale [cite46†19 ]. Our approach not only builds on the foundational research in prompt refinement and automated generation [cite47†20 ] but also sets itself apart by robustly highlighting the practical advantages of using SLMs.\nL80: ## III METHODS\nL81: \nL82: In this section, we present CPR, a simple yet effective framework for mitigating hallucinations induced by ill-formed prompts. We describe the details of our framework divided in the following subsections.\nL83: ### III-A Dataset Construction\nL84: We first construct a dataset to adapt an SLM to our specific tasks. Our training dataset for prompt refinement is constructed from three sources: the Wikipedia English dataset (WikiEn) [cite48†21 ], the Multi-domain Question Rewriting dataset (MQR) [cite49†22 ], and the Wikidata Description dataset (WikiD) [cite50†23 ]. We transform these into instruction fine-tuning form, which are pairs of prompts and ground-truth completions. Each dataset is formatted to train the model for three specific tasks as follows:\nL85: TABLE I: Comparison of hallucination index (HI), content quality score (CQS), and win rate (WR) between ill-formed of output quality from refined informative prompts upon different inference models. Bold values represent the best values.\nL86: Method  | Fine-tuned Models  | Inference Models\nL87: Llama-2 (7B) [cite51†24 ]  | GPT-3.5 [cite31†6 ]\nL88: HI ($\\downarrow$)  | CQS ($\\uparrow$)  | WR ($\\uparrow$)  | HI ($\\downarrow$)  | CQS ($\\uparrow$)  | WR ($\\uparrow$)\nL89: Original  | -  | 0.51  | 0.38  | -  | 0.16  | 0.51  | -\nL90: CPR w/o Descriptions  | Gemma (2B) [cite52†25 ]  | 0.35  | 0.51  | 0.51  | 0.14  | 0.58  | 0.61\nL91: Phi-2 (2.7B) [cite53†26 ]  | 0.34  | 0.54  | 0.53  | 0.12  | 0.63  | 0.64\nL92: Llama-2 (7B) [cite51†24 ]  | 0.32  | 0.62  | 0.73  | 0.10  | 0.68  | 0.78\nL93:  | Phi-3 (3.8B)  | 0.15  | 0.69  | 0.79  | 0.08  | 0.71  | 0.81\nL94:  | Qwen (1.5) (4B)  | 0.28  | 0.65  | 0.76  | 0.09  | 0.69  | 0.80\nL95:  | Zephyr (3B)  | 0.17  | 0.68  | 0.78  | 0.09  | 0.70  | 0.80\nL96:  | FLAN-T5 XL (2.85B)  | 0.31  | 0.60  | 0.74  | 0.10  | 0.67  | 0.75\nL97:  | Phi-3.5 mini (3B)  | 0.13  | 0.77  | 0  | 0  | 0  | 0\nL98:  | Llama 3.2 (3.21B)  | 0.16  | 0.78  | 0  | 0  | 0  | 0\nL99: CPR (Ours)  | Gemma (2B) [cite52†25 ]  | 0.17  | 0.59  | 0.84  | 0.07  | 0.68  | 0.92\nL100: Phi-2 (2.7B) [cite53†26 ]  | 0.14  | 0.63  | 0.85  | 0.09  | 0.73  | 0.93\nL101: Llama-2 (7B) [cite51†24 ]  | 0.13  | 0.75  | 0.92  | 0.04  | 0.83  | 0.96\nL102:  | Phi-3 (3.8B)  | 0.14  | 0.73  | 0.88  | 0.05  | 0.79  | 0.94\nL103:  | Qwen (1.5) (4B)  | 0.16  | 0.67  | 0.87  | 0.06  | 0.76  | 0.91\nL104:  | Zephyr (3B)  | 0.15  | 0.71  | 0.89  | 0.05  | 0.77  | 0.93\nL105:  | FLAN-T5 XL (2.85B)  | 0.19  | 0.65  | 0.85  | 0.07  | 0.74  | 0.88\nL106:  | Phi-3.5 mini (3B)  | 0.13  | 0.77  | 0  | 0  | 0  | 0\nL107:  | Llama 3.2 (3.21B)  | 0.16  | 0.78  | 0  | 0  | 0  | 0\nL108:   * •\nL109: WikiEn Dataset: This dataset comprises a comprehensive collection of English text from Wikipedia that meets high linguistic standards. Using the GPT-3.5 API, we verify and refine 10,000 entries to ensure they are free of punctuation errors, establishing a baseline of well-formed text. Simultaneously, we introduce punctuation errors into a copy of the 10,000 entries using the same API, creating a unique dataset of paired examples.\nL110: This setup is crucial for fine-tuning the model to improve text correction and punctuation accuracy.\nL111:   * •\nL112: \nL113: MQR Dataset: This dataset, consisting of 2,114 pairs of paraphrased questions, is crucial for enhancing the model’s skills in nuanced linguistic transformations like word substitution and paraphrasing. The MQR dataset aids in training the model to transform ill-formed prompts into clearer, more accurate questions, improving its ability to align with user intents and enhance interaction quality and output relevancy.\nL114: \nL115:   * •\nL116: WikiD Dataset: We selected a fraction of the Wikidata dataset based on lookup frequency, comprising 10,000 pairs of keywords and concise descriptions. This selection trains the model to generate accurate descriptions from minimal inputs and to enhance and expand on prompts, producing contextually relevant descriptions. Fine-tuning an SLM with WikiD equips the model to deliver enriched responses, significantly boosting performance in prompt-based tasks.\nL117: ### III-B Fine-tuning the Model\nL118: We then fine-tune an SLM with the constructed dataset. For the fine-tuning process, we incorporate instruction fine-tuning techniques [cite54†27 ] coupled with low rank adaptation (LoRA) [cite55†28 ] to enhance parameter efficiency. The approach of utilizing LoRA is specifically chosen to counteract the limitations associated with traditional full fine-tuning, notably its tendency to cause catastrophic forgetting by modifying every parameter of the model [cite56†29 ].\nL119: LoRA addresses this by selectively updating only a small subset of model parameters, thereby preserving the pre-trained strengths of the model while still adapting to new tasks.\nL120: By refining the model’s ability to handle specific instructional tasks without overhauling its entire parameter structure, we achieve a more targeted improvement in performance. This approach not only maintains the general capabilities of the model but also ensures greater efficiency and reduced resource consumption during the fine-tuning phase.\nL121: \nL122: Algorithm 1 Description Generation and Reranking\nL123: 1: Input: Input prompt $q$, maximum number of descriptions $m$, perplexity threshold $\\tau$, language model $f$, description $d$\nL124: \nL125: 2: Define Perplexity Function:\nL126: \nL127: 3: $PPL(d_{i})=exp[{-\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\log p(w_{j}|w_{j-1})}]$ $\\triangleright$ $N$: tokens in $d_{i}$, $p(w_{j}|w_{j-1})$: next-token probability\nL128: \nL129: 4: Generate and Rerank:\nL130: \nL131: 5: Initialize $D$ as an empty list\nL132: \nL133: 6: $i=0$\nL134: \nL135: 7: while $|D|<m$ do\nL136: \nL137: 8:   $d_{i}\\leftarrow f(q)$\nL138: \nL139: 9:   if $PPL(d_{i})>\\tau$ then\nL140: \nL141: 10:    break\nL142: \nL143: 11:   else\nL144: 12:    Append $d_{i}$ to $D$\nL145: \nL146: 13:   $i\\leftarrow i+1$\nL147: \nL148: 14: $D\\leftarrow\\text{sort}(D)$, Set key to $PPL(x)$\nL149: \nL150: 15: $D_{\\text{top}}\\leftarrow D[:k]$ $\\triangleright$ Select top-$k$ descriptions\nL151: \nL152: 16: return $D_{\\text{top}}$\nL153: ### III-C Cleaning and Paraphrasing Prompts\nL154: Using the fine-tuned SLM, ill-formed prompts are cleaned and rephrased into clear, well-formed versions. The model corrects linguistic inaccuracies such as grammar and syntax errors, a skill developed through extensive training on the Wiki dataset, which includes diverse text examples to enhance error detection and correction.\nL155: Additionally, it enhances clarity by rephrasing ambiguous phrases, benefiting from its training with the MQR dataset where it learned nuanced linguistic transformations like paraphrasing and word substitution. This dual capability enables the model to deliver prompts that are structurally sound and semantically clear.\nL156: ### III-D Informative Description Generation and Reranking\nL157: Merely cleaning the prompt still leaves it lacking in necessary contextual information, which hinders the ability to generate concise responses. This issue occurs because, while SLMs have the necessary information in their corpus, they often struggle to produce it without sufficient contextual guidance. To mitigate this problem, we enhance prompts by adding supplementary contextual information generated from the model.\nL158: This process is tailored to align with the refined prompts, improving the LLM’s processing effectiveness. Through targeted fine-tuning with our specialized dataset, the SLM learns to generate enriched descriptions that directly complement the cleaned prompts.\n"
